    "text": "# `merge_intervals` \u2014 Merging Overlapping Closed Integer Intervals\n\n```python\ndef merge_intervals(intervals):\n    \"\"\"\n    Merge overlapping closed integer intervals.\n\n    Args:\n        intervals: iterable of (start, end) pairs, where start <= end.\n\n    Returns:\n        A new sorted list of disjoint (start, end) tuples covering the\n        same set of integers as the input. The input is not mutated.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not intervals:\n        return []\n\n    # Copy + sort by start, then by end (stable, deterministic).\n    ordered = sorted((tuple(iv) for iv in intervals), key=lambda iv: (iv[0], iv[1]))\n\n    merged = [ordered[0]]\n    for start, end in ordered[1:]:\n        last_start, last_end = merged[-1]\n        if start <= last_end:                      # overlap or touch\n            if end > last_end:                     # extend if needed\n                merged[-1] = (last_start, end)\n        else:\n            merged.append((start, end))\n\n    return merged\n```\n\n## Why this works \u2014 the invariant\n\n**Invariant (holds before each iteration of the loop):**\n\n> `merged` is a list of pairwise-disjoint, sorted, closed intervals whose union equals the union of all intervals processed so far, and whose last element `(last_start, last_end)` has the **largest end** among all intervals seen so far.\n\nTwo facts make the invariant easy to maintain:\n\n1. **Sorting by start** guarantees",
